  "worked_solution": "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=\u27e80,0\u27e9.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e84t,3{t}^{2}-1\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (3{t}^{2}-1)\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}-t+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\u27e8-2,0\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e82(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{3}-0+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8-2,0\u27e9 & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e82{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=\u27e80,0\u27e9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e80,0\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2=2(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,\u21d2\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ {t}^{3}-t=t(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,\u21d2\\,t=\\pm 1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions that satisfy both equations are $t=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the particle is at the origin  when $t=\\pm 1.$",
